sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2024-collective-07> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "When a planarian is cut once, cells that were adjacent neighbours at the same positional-information value make a head on one side and a tail on the other, so this anatomical decision cannot be made locally: wound cells must coordinate with the rest of the fragment to learn where they are, which way they face and what structures remain.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Head-versus-tail choice at a planarian cut cannot be made locally";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "McMillen P, Levin M, “Collective intelligence: A unifying concept for integrating biology across scales and substrates”, Communications biology (2024), doi:10.1038/s42003-024-06037-4; section “Introduction › Left/right, head/tail: random, but collective anatomical decisions”. Scope: Regenerating planarian fragments"
}
